<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#fatwa">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatwa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A fatwa is a formal legal opinion issued by a qualified Islamic jurist. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> obtained fatwas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> jurists condemning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> as insufficiently observant, which served as religious justification for deposing them in the 1090s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fatwa.jpg" alt="Fatwa" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qasim ibn Husayn Shirazi (scribe) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tazkarat_al-Fuqaha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Seville in 1091, Badajoz in 1094 and Zaragoza in 1110, was accomplished through a combination of military pressure and legal justification, with Yusuf obtaining <b>fatwas</b> from Maliki jurists condemning the taifa rulers for impiety and illegal taxation before deposing them, a formula that framed conquest as religious correction.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…drew accusations of sponsoring terrorism, sharpened when fifteen of the September 11 hijackers proved to be Saudi nationals and answered by the Grand Mufti's 2014 <b>fatwa</b> against ISIS and al-Qaeda and the Islamic Military Counter Terrorism Coalition of 2015; since 2016 the state has stopped funding mosques abroad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1566 the Ottoman Sheikh al-Islam issued <b>fatwas</b> allowing the killing of Yazidis and the sale of their women as slaves.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ebussuud Efendi, şeyhülislam from 1545 to 1574, worked with Suleiman the Magnificent to reconcile the sultan’s law (kanun) with the sharia, and issued thousands of legal opinions (<b>fatwas</b>), on matters from land tenure to the drinking of coffee.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
